@how own mechanism: Co-Design the Call, Not Just the Project (source: this pattern's ! conclusion line; L10 rationale-backfill, zai-1, 2026-09-05)

! conclusion: Co-Design the Call, Not Just the Project

  + context: You are about to write the briefs that will determine what a cohort of researchers spends years working on.

  + IF:
    You are assembling a doctoral programme, a funding call, or a research agenda with academic and practice partners.

  + HOWEVER:
    Agenda-setting is almost always done by the few people writing the bid, and partners are brought in afterwards to endorse topics they had no hand in choosing. The co-design then happens inside individual projects, where the important decision — what is worth studying at all — has already been made.

  + THEN:
    Run the topic generation itself as an open process before the briefs exist. Invite opt-in topic descriptions from the whole academic community; theme them collaboratively on a shared board; narrow them through structured participatory selection (breakout groups, then transparent voting) rather than editorial judgement. Run a parallel session with practice partners and link their priorities back to the academic topics afterwards, so neither side simply ratifies the other.

  + BECAUSE:
    A topic bank generated this way is larger and stranger than any drafting group would produce, it is genuinely co-owned, and the surplus outlives the call — enough material to run several rounds, seed follow-on bids, and give partners a reason to stay. The goodwill comes from having actually decided together.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Budget the topic-generation workshop as a work package in the bid, not as pre-award goodwill.]
    next[Expect horse-trading over how topics seed into briefs, and agree the selection criteria (fit, novelty, boundary-pushing, partner relevance, likely difference made) before the vote, not after.]
    next[For a worked example across a three-university consortium, contact the AD[A]PT programme team in the School of Architecture.]
